 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to allowing disclosure of confidential HIV related information for research purposes to certain qualified researchers",
    "basePrintNo": "S7505",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7505-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2782, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7505\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto allowing disclosure of confidential HIV related information for\nresearch purposes to certain qualified researchers\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo allow disclosure of HIV/AIDs related medical information to\nqualified researchers who have received approval from a human research\nreview committee established and approved by the public health law or\na federally approved institutional review board (IRB) to conduct\nmedical research about the disease.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends PHL 2782 to add a new subdivision (r) which allows\nHIV/AIDS information to be disclosed to qualified researchers for\nmedical research purposes upon the approval of a research protocol by\na state human research review committee or by an institutional review\nboard.\n\nSection 2 provides for an effective date 120 days after enactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe current law disadvantages HIV-positive patients because it creates\na serious barrier to HIV/AIDS research by severely limiting the people\nwho are permitted to review medical records of HIV-positive patients\nand extract data for scientific research. Unlike research related to\nother diseases, only individuals providing health care to individuals\nwith HIV/AIDS are allowed to review the record and extract data;\nhowever, these care providers do not have time to extract data for\nresearch purposes. Thus, in many cases, research cannot be done\nbecause it is impossible to collect the necessary data. This bill will\nallow patients to experience the benefits that may accrue to them as\nresearch generates new knowledge about the disease. An illustration of\nthe research that would benefit from access to the records is a\ncomparison of the rates of liver cancer surveillance in patients\nreceiving care from hepatologists versus other physicians. This work\nwould allow researchers to determine whether these patients receive\nthe same level of care for liver disease as HIV-negative patients with\nliver disease. Many HIV-positive patients with liver disease receive\nmost of their care from infectious disease specialists and primary\ncare physicians. It would benefit HIV-positive patients to have a high\nimpact study demonstrating whether their physicians are adhering to\nguidelines about liver disease management. A comparison of rates of\nreferral for liver transplantation evaluation in HIV-positive versus\nHIV-negative patients could demonstrate whether these patients are\nbeing given equal access to liver transplantation, a life saving\nprocedure, or if their providers are failing to refer them for liver\ntransplantation evaluation in a timely manner.\n\nExpanded access to confidential AIDS records would allow researchers\nto learn more about how AIDS interacts with many medical conditions\nand will promote increased precision in the treatment of AIDS\npatients, a goal consistent with the 2015 Blueprint to End the\nEpidemic issued by the Department of Health (DOH).\n\nAmong other actions, the 2015 Blueprint recommends that the DOH expand\nthe use of existing AIDS data. Although AIDS information is routinely\ncollected in New York, it is not always used to better understand the\nAIDS epidemic, to improve patient outcomes, or to prevent new\ninfections This proposal directly addresses this Blueprint\nrecommendation, as data will be more thoroughly used for research that\n\ncould help better understand AIDS and eventually improve patient\noutcomes.\n\nIn order to maintain confidentiality, research access would be through\nand Institutional Review Board (IRB) established under state or\nfederal law. IRBs have the authority to approve human subject research\nsubject to regulations issued by Secretary of Health and Human\nServices and the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). In order to\napprove research, an IRB must determine that specified criteria have\nbeen satisfied, including the presence of adequate provisions to\nprotect the privacy of subjects and to maintain the confidentiality of\ndata.\n\nNew York law also protects human subjects in medical research projects\nnot otherwise subject to federal law through requiring public and\nprivate institutions and agencies engaging in human subject research\nto create human research review committees. Current New York law does\nnot permit disclosure of information about HIV/AIDS disease to\nqualified researchers approved either by an IRB or a human research\nreview committees. Only direct providers of health care are permitted\nto review such medical records and extract data for scientific\nresearch.\n\nAlthough HIV/AIDS related information is treated very restrictively\nunder the public health law  recent amendments to the statute reflect\na trend to de-stigmatize the disease in certain provisions of the law\nwhich simplify the HIV counseling and the testing process,\nregularizing them so that they are more like routine testing for other\ndiseases. This bill is consistent with that trend of treating HIV/AIDS\nlike other diseases by permitting qualified researchers to conduct\napproved medical research in much the same way as is done for other\ndiseases.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n120 days after the date on which the bill becomes law\n",
